WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.790
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:06.340
“Hi tree on the corner of Punt Road and
Alexandra Avenue and that little street that

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.440
goes up the side,
How are you?

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.720
How old are you?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.260
I’ve always wondered about you ever since
my slightly strange driving instructor (who

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:18.080
always smelled like cat food and peppermints)
told me you were his favourite tree.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.750
I hope they don’t knock you down.”

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:25.210
This was an email written to a tree in Melbourne,
Australia…

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.930
yes a tree.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:32.540
To combat the steady decline of trees in Melbourne,
following a 13-year drought, the city devised

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.420
a genius plan called the “Urban Forest Strategy”.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.020
Was their genius plan to invest in better
irrigation systems to keep the city’s trees

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.040
watered in the intense Aussie heat?

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:47.160
No… that’s boring …they instead gave
each and every tree in Melbourne an email

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.829
address, that’s 77,000 trees.

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:56.060
The idea was that Melbourne residents could
report tree vandalism, broken branches or

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.809
other issues that could threaten the tree’s
wellbeing, to the city council, by directly

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:02.860
emailing the tree itself.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.110
Sounds great!

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.060
But it went very wrong, in the most amazing
way possible.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:12.240
People started to write love letters to their
favourite trees.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.729
Since the scheme went live Melbourne City
Council have received thousands of love letters

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:22.799
via email, from all over the world, addressed
to the newly-famous trees of Melbourne.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.649
If you see a Melbournian tree you like on
Google Street View you can go to the project’s

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:30.289
website and find its email.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:35.259
As I can imagine happened when a Brush Box
tree received the following email from a mysterious

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:37.079
gentleman in Russia:

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:38.770
“Hello, dear Tree.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:44.679
I read about this wonderful project and suppose
to write you from another side of Earth - Russia.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:47.689
I hope you have a good care and don’t sick.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.499
One day we will meet, may be.”

00:01:50.499 --> 00:01:54.479
Oh Vladimir, I never knew you were such a
lover of nature.

00:01:54.479 --> 00:02:00.609
Also, an elm tree received this email from
a fellow 15,000 kilometres away in jolly-olde

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:03.469
England, it’s not from me, honest:

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:08.640
“Dear Elm, I was delighted to find you alive
and flourishing, because a lot of your family

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.780
used to live in the UK, but they all caught
a terrible infection and died.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.550
Do be very careful, and if you notice any
unfamiliar insects e-mail an arboriculturist

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:19.659
at once.

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:24.030
I miss your characteristic silhouettes and
beautifully shaped branches — used to be

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.000
one of the glories of the English landscape
— more than I can say.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.849
Melbourne must be a beautiful city.

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:32.450
Sincere good wishes, D”

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:38.799
The trees have received every kind of email
from quick greetings, love letters and poems,

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:43.989
questions about current events and politics
and even existential dilemmas.

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:48.780
One gentleman called Troy was so concerned
about the Greek debt crisis that he had a

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.790
full-on political debate with a local Red
Cedar tree.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.780
And yes, the tree actually emailed back, as
they often do.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:56.950
Troy opened with:

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.510
“Hi Tree,
Are you worried about being affected by the

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:01.950
Greek debt crisis?

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.900
Should Greece be allowed to stay in the European
Union?

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.689
Regards,
Troy”

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:08.120
To which the tree replied:

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.290
“Hi Troy,
I seem to remember the Greeks razed you to

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.310
the ground one time—are you still angry
at them?

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:18.200
Greece is not out of the woods yet, but may
be out of the EU….

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.620
Some say that they should be allowed to devalue
their currency in order to recover their economy,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.379
but the EU will not allow them to do that.

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:29.859
Some say that it is partly the austerity program,
which has made it this bad.

00:03:29.859 --> 00:03:34.159
They say austerity was a disaster for Russia
after the breakup of the Soviet Union and

00:03:34.159 --> 00:03:36.909
for the recovery of Asia from the GFC…

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:39.739
I don’t know, but then I’m only a tree.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:42.620
Regards,
Western Red Cedar”

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.680
Overall the project has been a huge success
and despite the unintended consequences, being

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.430
able to email trees has vastly increased the
citizens of Melbourne’s interest in looking

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.459
after and increasing the amount of trees in
Melbourne.

00:03:56.459 --> 00:04:01.469
Having canopy cover from thousands of trees
decreases the average temperature of the entire

00:04:01.469 --> 00:04:05.200
city by 4 degrees Celsius in the summer.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.569
Which is needed as it’s bloody Australia.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:12.260
I just hope residents don’t become too attached
to their local trees as I fear for what they

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.969
might do to them.

00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:18.799
One golden elm tree already has a stalker,
who’s latest email read:

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:24.229
“Dear 1037148, you deserve to be known by
more than a number.

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:25.630
I love you.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:26.880
Always and forever.”

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:35.470
…yeah, this whole thing could start to get
really weird.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:54.220
Thanks for watching.

